Mike Kelly wrote:
> Flavio Cardone (wafna) wrote:

> > I have some questions regarding ascension kits / GDSM.I read here that
> > practically everyone favours having GDSM.

> GDSM or SDSM are the only types of DSM that confer a significant
> intrinsic that can't be gained somewhere else (e.g. by eating the
> appropriately colored dragon). Which one is better depends on what
> other kit you happen to have lying around. You definately want both
> magic resistance and reflection.

> Of course if I happen to have both of these from other sources I'm
> quite happy wearing DSM of whatever color happens to leave scales first
> for the AC.

Not to mention that it is very light weight and doesn't hinder
spell casting.  On at least one occasion, I switched from
dwarven mithral to dragon scales, even without a scroll on hand
to make them into mail, simply for those reasons.  (Admittedly,
on that occasion, I had some pretty good other armor, so that I
was still at around AC -10 or -12.  And I was playing a spell
casting role, and could cast protection at 0% failure, so that I
could get the extra AC when I needed it.)

Other than that, I've found having a robe for spell casting to
be invaluable, unless you're a XP30 wizard or something like
that.  And wearing a robe means GDSM for magic resistance, which
means amulet of reflection for reflection.  At least, that's
what works for me.

> >After I was killed by a black D
> > the first time I reached the castle, I prefer having BDSM, with magic
> > resistance from a cloak, reflection from a shield, and levitation from a
> > ring...

> Reflection guards against disintegration blasts. You can also eat a
> black dragon for disintegration resistance.

Eating the black dragon won't protect your armor.  Generally,
unless you're caught in the middle of a long straight passage,
you should have no trouble avoiding disintegration once you know
the dragon is there.  (It won't use its breath attack if you're
adjacent to it.  This is one of the cases where melee fighting
beats ranged attacks.)  But learning that it is there can cost
you a shield or a helmet.

> >I also prefer (I only ascended Valkyries) Excalibur over Mjollnir
> > because of level-drain resistance - but is there any other way to have
> > level-drain resistance?

> Wielding Stormbringer or the Staff of Aesculapius. Or polymorphing into
> an undead/demon. Level-drain resisance is near meaningless if you have
> magic cancellation 3 (you really should). Only one in several hundred
> attacks will actually drain your level.

> Both weapons are fine with regards to dealing damage (although Mjollnir
> is rather weak vs shock resistant monsters).

Excalibur is also automatic searching, which is nice.  And it's
easy to get if you're a lawful.  About the only thing I'd put
above it is Grayswandir---or Sunsword if I have no other light
source.  With the exception of Wizard, the guaranteed first
gifts are not that cool; they actually guarantee that you won't
get something more interesting.  For lawfuls, for example, they
guarantee that you won't get Grayswandir, and in no case are as
good as Excalibur, which you can easily get otherwise.  (For a
neutral Valk, Mjollnir probably isn't that bad, but it does mean
that you won't get Magicbane, Frost Brand or Fire Brand.)

